  "worked_solution": "Given that the implication $S⇒N$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- $S$ being true *guarantees* that $N$ must also be true. Therefore, $S$ is a *sufficient condition* for $N.$\n\n- For $S$ to be true, it is *necessary* that $N$ is true. Therefore, $N$ is a *necessary condition* for $S.$ However, note that $N$ being true does *not* guarantee that $S$ is true.\n\nThese ideas can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nIn our case, we need to find the conditions that are *sufficient* for the given one:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,(|n-2|=5)\n$$\n\nNote that the solutions to the given equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|n-2| & =5 \\\\ n-2 & =\\pm 5 \\\\ n & =-3,7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given conditions.\n\n- Condition I is a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(n=7)\\,⇒\\,(|n-2|=5)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nIf $n=7,$ then $|n-2|=5.$\n\n- Condition II is *not* a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(n\\ge -3)\\,⇏\\,(|n-2|=5)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, if $n=0,$ then  $n\\ge -3$, but\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|n-2| & =|0-2| \\\\  & =|-2| \\\\  & \\ne 5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Condition III is a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n(n+3=0)\\,⇒\\,(|n-2|=5)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nIf $n+3=0,$ then $n=-3$ and $|n-2|=5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
